// Practice quiz: linear and binary search
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, so comparisons are written in words.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/arrays-lists-and-maps/linear-and-binary-search

::o1 first match::A linear search returns the index of the first element equal to the target, or -1. Searching int[] a \= \{4, 7, 9, 7\} for 7 returns what? {
=1#Correct. The scan stops at the first 7, which is at index 1.
~3#Not quite. That is the last 7; the scan stops as soon as it finds the first one.
~7#Not quite. The search returns where the target is, not the target itself.
~2#Not quite. Index 2 holds 9; the 7 before it is found first.
}

::o1 when to give up::In a linear search, when is it safe to return -1? {
=Only after every element has been checked without a match#Correct. Until then the target could still be further along.
~As soon as one element does not match#Not quite. That gives up at the first mismatch, even when the target is later in the array.
~When the target is bigger than the first element#Not quite. A linear search makes no assumption about order, so this proves nothing.
~Never; it should return the array length instead#Not quite. The method promises -1 for a missing target.
}

::o2 first middle::Binary search on int[] a \= \{3, 8, 12, 15, 21, 30, 44\} for 30 starts with low 0 and high 6. Which index does it check first? {
=3, the middle of 0 and 6#Correct. (0 + 6) / 2 is 3, so it compares 30 with a[3], which is 15.
~0, the start of the array#Not quite. That is how a linear search begins.
~5, where 30 is#Not quite. Binary search does not know where 30 is; it starts in the middle.
~6, the end of the array#Not quite. It checks the middle so that it can discard half.
}

::o2 narrowing::Binary search is looking for 30 and finds a[mid] is 15, at mid 3, with low 0 and high 6. What happens next? {
=low becomes 4, because 30 is greater than 15#Correct. Everything at index 3 or left of it is 15 or less, so the search keeps indexes 4 to 6.
~high becomes 2, because 30 is greater than 15#Not quite. That keeps the left half, where every value is 15 or less.
~low becomes 3#Not quite. Index 3 was already checked and is not 30, so it can be left out.
~It reports 30 missing#Not quite. Indexes 4 to 6 could still hold 30.
}

::o3 unsorted input::Binary search runs on int[] a \= \{9, 2, 30, 4, 1\} looking for 4. What can happen? {
=It may report 4 missing even though it is there#Correct. This run checks 30, then 9, and discards the part holding 4. Discarding half is safe only when the values are in ascending order.
~It always finds 4, just more slowly#Not quite. On unsorted input it can discard the half that holds the target.
~Java throws an exception for unsorted arrays#Not quite. Nothing checks the order; the answer is simply unreliable.
~It sorts the array first#Not quite. Binary search never changes the array.
}

::o3 step growth::A sorted array has about 1,000 elements, and binary search needs at most about 10 checks. About how many does it need at most for 2,000 elements? {
=About 11#Correct. Doubling the size adds only one more halving step.
~About 20#Not quite. Doubling the count of checks would fit a linear search, not binary search.
~About 2,000#Not quite. That is the worst case for checking elements one by one.
~Still exactly 10#Not quite. The extra halving step is needed for the larger array.
}
